  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A slow moving cold front crosses the area \nFriday with the heaviest rain possible during the morning rush \nhour.\n\nAn upper-level trough pivoting over the Great Lakes region will\ncontinue to slowly track eastward today before the upper low \nmoves overhead on Saturday. At the surface, this translates to a\nslow moving cold front crawling across parts of our area today.\n\nIsolated to widely scattered showers early this morning will \nbecome more widespread by daybreak, making for a wet morning\nrush hour. Since there has been a lack of rain for our area the\npast 2 to 3 weeks, the showers are rather beneficial. Rainfall \namounts will vary across the area with lower amounts N/W (a \ntenth to three- quarters of an inch) while southern NJ and \nDelmarva will have higher totals of 1 to 2 inches with locally \nheavier amounts possible. Along the I-95 corridor, the latest \nruns of hi res guidance has increased slightly, up to 1.5 inches\nwith locally higher amounts of up to 2 inches possible. The \nrain is expected to occur over a long enough period to preclude \nany widespread flooding concerns, but some localized poor\ndrainage flooding is possible. The WPC has added a Marginal Risk\nfor Excessive Rainfall along the I-95 corridor and southeast\ntoday. A few thunderstorms may develop in the morning, but the \nchances of this are lower, around 20-40%. The front stalls for a\nperiod around or just southeast of the I-95 corridor during the\nmorning before resuming a SE push during the afternoon. This \nwill promote the higher QPF totals for those areas with \nmoderately heavy showers and perhaps an isolated thunderstorm \npossible during the morning rush hour. Conditions will gradually\nimprove throughout the day with mostly fair weather for areas \nN/W. There will be slower improvement for S/E areas. For the \nI-95 corridor, conditions are forecast to improve during the \nafternoon. The SE areas may not see any substantial improvement \nuntil the evening hours.\n\nThe cold front departs offshore Friday night, with dry conditions \nreturning for much of the area. However, guidance indicates an \nupper- low will track eastward across northern portions of the \narea on Saturday. This will lead to a lingering chance for some \nlight rain north of I-78, with the highest chances (40-50%) \nacross the Poconos and far northern NJ. By Sunday morning, the \nupper-low will be east of the area bringing a return of dry \nconditions to the region along with cooler temperatures.",
